<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dispositio Achillea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1473</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dispositio Achillea was the house law issued in 1473 by Albert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, to regulate the Hohenzollern succession. It laid down that the Mark of Brandenburg with the electoral dignity should pass undivided to the eldest son, while the Franconian lands around Ansbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> could go to younger sons. It helped keep Brandenburg from being partitioned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dispositio-achillea.jpg" alt="Dispositio Achillea" width="190" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Känsterle at Dutch Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlbrechtAchilles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
